#
# The named-rule spelling of `undecidable_nested_gate.guard`, which behaved differently from the
# parameterized one and was worse.
#
# `inner_gate` is a plain rule, so it is also a top-level rule and is evaluated on its own. Its
# nested `when` condition cannot be answered, so as an assertion it fails -- and that failure exits
# the file 19 whatever happens to `guarded`. The exit code therefore says nothing here, which is why
# the test asserts on `guarded` itself.
#
# What happened before the rule-status cache was keyed on the reference's role: `inner_gate` was
# evaluated once, as an assertion, and cached under its name alone. The gate reference read that
# cached FAIL rather than re-evaluating with gate semantics, `eval_rule` read a non-PASS condition as
# "rule does not apply", and `guarded` was reported SKIP with `Encrypted` unchecked. The merge-base
# reports `guarded` FAIL here, so this was a regression the exit code hid.
#
rule inner_gate {
    when Enabled !EMPTY {
        Enabled == true
    }
}

rule guarded when inner_gate {
    MustBeTrue == true
}
